{
 "ticket": "RYA-1213",
 "finding": "all 51 of the 51 Reference Grade products carry xi_state NOT_IN_CAMPAIGN: the RYA-1120 perturb-and-re-derive campaign's run unit is (ion x holding x tier x treatment x route) and REFERENCE is a new tier, so no dA/dxi exists for any of these pools.",
 "why_it_matters": "sigma_reported omits the xi term rather than carrying a zero, so every Reference bar is a LOWER BOUND -- which the feed states on each row in `sigma_reported_caveat`. The consequence is that a Reference product can look like the most precise measurement of its cell while being the least completely budgeted. The sharpest case is the flagship: VIS Fe I kurucz2005 1D-LTE reports 0.0524 on 175 lines against its Deep sibling's 0.0862 and Codex sibling's 0.0836 -- and its own sigma_syst (0.0504) is LARGER than Deep's (0.0402), so the gf half of the budget is honest and the whole difference is the absent xi term.",
 "what_was_done_about_it": "NOTHING TO THE VALUES. The website's 'best-constrained' pick now ranks budget COMPLETENESS before width, so an incomplete bar cannot win a headline -- which it had already been doing on the red-optical Fe I page before this ticket. The term itself is an OWED MEASUREMENT: closing it means running RYA-1120's campaign on the REFERENCE tier, which is a separate piece of work.",
 "the_bracket_is_ILLUSTRATIVE_and_NOT_ADOPTED": "\ud83d\udd34 THE SIBLING DERIVATIVE IS NOT THIS POOL'S. `line_set_for_product`'s own rule is that a campaign key is never inherited from a twin, and RYA-1114 F2 measured why: one dA/dxi served to products measured on DIFFERENT line pools is how a wrong number gets published as if it were measured. VIS is the worst case for inheritance -- Reference there is 175 lines against Codex's 67 and Deep's 108, a genuinely different population. The column below shows what the bar WOULD read if a sibling's derivative were adopted, purely so the size of the gap is visible. It is not a proposed value.",
